  client-side-local and you later want teammates, it's a rewrite: storage, identity, change propagation, and the
  notification routing all get replaced. When one direction is a downgrade and the other is a teardown, pick the one you
  can walk back.

  [1mIt matches what you actually wrote.[0m "Users get notified when tasks [3mthey[0m care about change" — plural users, and "care
  about" only means something when someone [3melse[0m might change the task. If you'd meant reminders, the natural phrasing is
  "remind me when something's due." I'd rather design to your words than to the cheapest build.

  The YAGNI cut that keeps this from ballooning: [1mmulti-user semantics without authentication.[0m A server, a shared task
  list, and users who identify by picking a name — no passwords, no sessions, no password reset, no email verification.
  Real auth is a large chunk of work, it's completely orthogonal to notifications, and it bolts on later without
  touching the notification design. That keeps the substrate thin while still being genuinely multi-user.

  I'd also [1mdefer system events[0m (due dates, recurrence). They're purely additive — once a notification pipeline exists, a
  scheduled job becomes just another producer feeding it. Nothing about building them later is harder than building
  them now.

  So the slice is: small server + shared tasks + name-only identity + notifications on other people's changes. Delivery
  channel (in-app bell vs email vs push) is the next question — I'm not assuming it yet.
